 dkrieger@netcom.com  (Dave Krieger) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-11-215:
>>From: Phil Goetz <goetz@cs.Buffalo.EDU>
>>2.  Natural ecosystems are being disrupted, species eradicated, and
>>the environment polluted at rates beyond optimal.
>
>Nope, sorry.  The evidence in favor of this hypothesis is weak, sporadic,
>over-reported, and over-extrapolated.  (1) Atmospheric CO2 levels have
>failed to rise anywhere near as fast as trumpeted by doomsayers.  _Science

 Disproving the extreme forecasts doesn't amount to an argument against
the mainstream predictions that undesirable climatic changes will become
apparent sometime in the next century. The mainstream forecasts seem to
be a reasonable guess about what would happen if the singularity doesn't
happen, and provide a strong case that CO2 emissions exceed optimal levels.
It should not be surprising that an unowned region such as the atmosphere
suffers from a "tragedy of the commons".

>Furthermore, if you were Gaia, would you be _pro_ or _con_ an increase in
>CO2?  Okay, you're a planet-girdling life-form.  Your metabolism runs on
>CO2, water, and sunlight (i.e., photosynthesis).  Plants take up CO2 and
...
>Ice Age out of Europe, for example.)  Lovely!  Your ecosystems fizz with
>more CO2, enabling you to grow more plants, produce more sugars, feed more
>animals, generate more species.  What a bargain!  (And now some of these
>animals are talking about _reproducing_ you elsewhere in space!  Such a
>deal!)

 Gaia is not an entity capable of being pro or con anything. There will
certainly be species which benefit from increased CO2, but it is likely
that more humans will be hurt than helped. 

>We're overdue for the next Ice Age by about 2,000 years, according to the
>geological record -- what, oh what, could be holding it back?  "Button up

 Last I heard, no serious climatologist thought ice ages were predictable
enough for that to mean anything.

>>I'm not really talking about government vs. anarchy, but about the values
>>of individuals.  If most people agree that city life is good enough,
>>the minority that disagrees will be screwed, whether by central
>>decision or by local action.
>
>WHAT??  That ain't how supply and demand works.  If most people agree that
>city life is good enough, the price of land out in the boonies drops to
>nothing.  Your point here, if you have one, is completely opaque to me.

 If the supply of land is finite and population growth continues, cities
will expand to occupy the entire planetary surface. If technology fails
to create adequate substitutes for Earth's surface, this will be a big
problem for those of us who don't want to live in cities.
